				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\mathbb{E}}(H)=n\frac{M}{N}$$\end{document}E(H)=nMN. In CARA, the number of hits H in the population and the number of token samples n are both defined by a threshold p (1% or 5%), that is, M = ⌈pN⌉, n = ⌈pN⌉, where ⌈⋅⌉ stands for the ceil operation. Therefore, we have \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
